  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Hare's ear (Otidea onotica) is a small, cup-shaped fungus belonging to the Otideaceae family, characterized by its distinctive ear-like or funnel-shaped fruiting bodies. Found in New England forests, this delicate mushroom is a saprotroph that plays a role in forest decomposition.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Ear-like shape",
      "description": "Distinctive asymmetrical, ear or hare-shaped fruiting body that is typically tan to pale brown in color",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Interior surface",
      "description": "The inner surface is smooth and paler than the exterior, often with a slightly wrinkled appearance",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Size and texture",
      "description": "Small fruiting bodies, typically 2-4 cm tall, with a delicate, somewhat translucent appearance when fresh",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Hare's ear thrives on moist soil rich in decaying wood and organic matter in deciduous and mixed forests throughout New England, particularly in spring and early summer.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The common name 'hare's ear' refers to the distinctive shape of the fungus, which resembles the long, pointed ear of a hare, making it one of the most visually memorable cup fungi in the forest.",
